essure to being  the one changed by it you just need to  have the right sort of pressure with the  correct motivations  [Music]  the Nemec arced directly after the fight  with Goku on earth can be seen as the  first time Vegeta really started to  garner some emotional depth and range as  a character typically speaking before  you introduce a character into a story  whether it be a villain hero mentor etc  most writers will tell you it's  important to fully understand the  background of and know the personality  of the character first and in the case  of Vegeta it was important to fully  understand what he wants what he needs  and what he was capable of as a  character these are specific topics I've  covered in other videos like my one on  trunks so if you haven't seen that  already check it out the thing your  character wants will almost always be  something external something physical  he's trying to solve his inner emptiness  with external solutions he thinks that  if he could just get this one thing  everything will be perfect that he'll be  powerful respected and most importantly  fulfilled because at the moment  he most definitely is not happy now  oftentimes with characters like this the  things that they want could be perfectly  respectable goals now in the case of  Vegeta universe white domination isn't  exactly reasonable but nevertheless  issues arise when these characters are  pursuing goals that are furthering their  enslavement to the lies they hold to be  true which leads me to what Vegeta's  character actually needs now this is the  part a lot of novice writers forget  about and it's the part that gives the  most emotional depth to a character so  at this point the story Vegeta has been  living by this toxic misconception and  misconception over what was truly  important something that holds him back  and something that prevents him from  growing emotionally now the antidote to  this in a word simply is the truth that  it doesn't make you weak to care about  someone or show affection for them in a  more general sense the thing your  character will need oftentimes won't be  physical it won't be material usually  hinging on so emotional characteristic  being denied or pushed to the wayside in  Vegeta's case it was empathy and love I  mean it wouldn't be much of a story of  all Vegeta's misconception was was  physical with it being an emotional  abstract concept it lends credence to  the likelihood of him not noticing it  and having to be taught to embrace it  later however because emotional growth  isn't exactly a tangible thing and  really can't be seen as a writer you  need a physical embodiment or  manifestation of the mo  Grosse Vegeta in this case undergoes  this is why we have scenes like his  death anomic where go coup arrives  demonstrating to him all of his mistakes  and trunks been shot by cells showing  him that he really does care about his  son it's nothing more than a  split-second realization in a number of  cases all delivered by consistent  pressure being applied to this specific  area of his psyche however the most  interesting part of this characters  growth has to be how it's all tied  together in Dragon Ball Z near the end  of his story in the final saga of the  show Vegeta is asked as a character to  choose between what he really wants and  what he actually needs in his specific  case he's asked to choose between the  ultimate power he wants to destroy Goku  and the family and loved ones he  actually needs this is the reason why  for many Vegeta's returned to the  dioxide in the Buu saga was so powerful  and compelling through years of great  terrific character building we fully  understood what Vegeta wanted and we  knew he was making a massive mistake in  taking this course of action however we  could all understand his thought process  in doing so this is tremendously  compelling character running and it's  largely why Vegeta was the best  character by far in my estimation during  the boos  for those that are interested in a more  complex nuance of character writing  Vegeta underwent during this section of  the story  a miniature negative change arc  something that's specifically called a  full arc in this arc the character  starts out just as he would in a  positive change arc already entrenched  in the line but unlike the positive  change arc in which he would eventually  overcome the light and embrace the truth  the protagonist in the fall arc will  reject every chance for embracing the  truth and will fall more and more deeply  into the madness of his own sins usually  dragging others along with them his  story will end in insanity obsessive  immorality or death and this was what  made Dragon Ball Z's Vegeta so  captivating because death doesn't carry  as much weight as it would in other  shows which normally is a bad thing  because of its perceived shortcomings  and stakes the upside that we get to see  in this instance is that we get to mix  and meld varying character archetypes  together resulting in Vegeta redeeming  himself in the afterlife resulting in a  completely fulfilled positive change  character arc by the end having admitted  to be lesser than Goku as a fighter but  realizing that he is just as rich as he  in the areas that actually matter and  that was the power of Dragon Balls  writing back in the day I believe  through and through Vegeta was the most  consistently compelling and well-written  character the entire series ever put to  paper now let's see how Dragon Ball  super handled it one of the major things  people enjoyed about Vegeta in the  original Dragon Ball story was that you  never really knew what would happen with  him he had this impulsive mentality that  promoted moments of great tension in  short he was an active car to that while  he was on the screen or had something to  say or do he would be moving the story  Ford to the benefit or to the detriment  of the main cast depending on how he was  feeling  however in Dragon Ball super he is very  much different very different and  strangely enough he's different because  he doesn't change he doesn't grow much  at all oddly enough he's different  because he relentlessly persists in  staying the exact same way he has been  since the beginning and this is in  essence because the structure of Dragon  Ball super was exceptionally unambitious  flat and at times somewhat vapid now  don't get me wrong Dragon Ball super has  delivered some extraordinary  strong episodes like episode 47 which  deliver tons of character rich dialogue  setups and debuts however the very  person who wrote that episode a to hero  Tomioka also wrote episode 67 which was  widely considered one of the most  uninspired confused rushed and poorly  executed endings the entire series has  seen while doing research for this video  I found a terrific quote what the novice  writer mistakes for craft is simply his  unconscious absorption of story elements  from every novel film or play he's ever  encountered as they write they match  their work by trial and error against a  model built up from accumulated reading  and watching the unschooled writer calls  this instant but it's merely habit and  it's rigidly limiting leading to them  either imitating their mental prototype  or imagines themselves in the oven guard  and repels against it what this means is  when someone new to the series watches  Dragonball Z they leave believing it's  successful because of climaxes like  Gohan vs. L or sacrifices like pickle  and Vegeta have made but in reality it's  the structure that laid the groundwork  for those instances to matter as much as  they did and what happens when you  remove all of the emotional meaning and  weight behind an action hoping that it  on its own is enough to make the scene  amazing you get scenes like this or if  you want another example you get this  moreover the very presence and failure  of these scenes ability to stand on  their own or to live up to the standard  of the originals that they're based on  only serve to highlight Dragon Ball  super shortcomings more the interest and  all that came from the original scenes  these were based on did not come from  the simple spectacle it provided so  readily but from the emotional meaning  and change of heart it represented in  the characters acting these scenes out  to expand on this point further I'd like  to take a look at this quote from Hayao  Miyazaki the major source for Japanese  animation is manga whose greatest  characteristic is its method of  expression centred on emotions in order  to express emotions space and time are  freely distorted this was a quote taken  from a larger interview Miyazaki  conducted as he lamented and explained  his thoughts on the popularity anime has  garnered in the West and across the  globe I'm a huge admirer of Miyazaki's  work and animation philosophy my  interpretation of this quote is that  animes X  pression of emotion is so potent because  of its grand sensational sense of scope  afforded to it by its stories due to the  nature of its medium in other words when  someone is sad angry or happy in anime  it's accentuated to deliver the  strongest impact it can and that's the  difference between Dragon Ball super and  Dragon Ball Z's Vegeta Hayao Miyazaki  much like myself and many others don't  care much for spectacle unless there's  an emotional weight behind it and it's  for that reason I care much more for  Vegeta's character during the Buu saga  that I did during his time in the  tournament of power it is for that very  reason I am interested keenly in this  moment from Vegeta's story and I'm not  interested at all in this one from  Dragon Ball super to further bolster  this line of criticism for those that  might disagree with it I ask you what  has changed about Vegeta or his current  situation since the end of battle of  gods the answer is very very little and  the troubles with that can be seen very  easily all of the Gena's monumental  moments in this series are rooted in  this archetype of change often times  positive change and sometimes a negative  one but a change nonetheless he isn't  forced to change in any meaningful  emotional way in Dragon Ball super  but the Reuters place him in  circumstances that are reminiscent of  his highlights in Dragon Ball Z in the  hopes that they will achieve what they  did for him in the past but they won't  they can't structurally speaking  narrative Lee speaking and logically  speaking they can't work the same way  because they are in the most fundamental  ways different all the major moments we  love from Dragon Ball Z that Vegeta  facilitated happened to coincide with  key moments from within the structure of  a character his Eagle focus returned to  destroy 19 his defense of his son trunks  his sacrifice everything he did be it  good or bad was informed first and  foremost by the character arc structure  he was a part of the moral arc in the  manga is somewhat interesting for some  of the reasons I spoke of today but also  others I plan to touch on when I review  that arc after it's finished but in  short while the manga handles his  character differently than the anime  does with some major changes it always  finishes the same way this is the first  arc that has led the way in quite some  time in the mangas and I'm very  interested to see the position  characters take and the consequences of  the arc to take a hold of the story once  all  is said and done in conclusion from my  perspective Dragon Ball super anime  feels exceptionally shallow in a lot of  areas there's zero effort made to create  a story that flows outside of its arcs  bar 1 or 2 easily missed moments Vegeta  Gohan piccolo and many other characters  in Dragon Ball affirm much of their  success through emotional growth and  struggle as I was applied in believable  original and specific ways over the  course of a long winding narrative  Vegeta acting as he does in Super will  never achieve what it did for him in the  original story  it's tone-deaf it's amateurish and it's  the perfect recipe for what became  Dragon Ball super z' boring sequel  thanks for watching  [Music]

